## **🎯 THREAT LANDSCAPE**

### **Threat Actors**


| **Type**            | **Motivation**               | **Example**                     | **Defense**                                        |
| ------------------- | ---------------------------- | ------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| **Nation-State**    | Espionage, disruption.       | APT29 (Cozy Bear).              | Advanced threat hunting, government collaboration. |
| **Criminal Groups** | Financial gain.              | Ransomware gangs (e.g., REvil). | Backups, MFA, user training.                       |
| **Hacktivists**     | Ideological reasons.         | Anonymous.                      | Monitor social media, DDoS protection.             |
| **Insiders**        | Revenge, profit, or mistake. | Disgruntled employee.           | Least privilege, monitoring, audits.               |
| **Script Kiddies**  | Boredom, bragging.           | Using pre-made tools.           | Basic security hygiene (patches, firewalls).       |


### **Malware Types**


| **Type**       | **Description**                                | **Example**     | **Detection**                  |
| -------------- | ---------------------------------------------- | --------------- | ------------------------------ |
| **Virus**      | Attaches to files; spreads when executed.      | ILOVEYOU virus. | Antivirus signatures.          |
| **Worm**       | Self-replicating; spreads without user action. | WannaCry.       | Network traffic analysis.      |
| **Trojan**     | Disguised as legitimate software.              | Emotet.         | Behavioral analysis.           |
| **Ransomware** | Encrypts files; demands payment.               | Ryuk, LockBit.  | File integrity monitoring.     |
| **Spyware**    | Secretly monitors user activity.               | FinFisher.      | EDR tools (e.g., CrowdStrike). |
| **Rootkit**    | Hides malware; gives admin access.             | Stuxnet.        | Memory forensics.              |
| **Botnet**     | Network of infected devices.                   | Mirai.          | Traffic to C2 servers.         |


### **Social Engineering Tactics**


| **Tactic**         | **Description**                        | **Example**                                      | **Defense**                           |
| ------------------ | -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------ | ------------------------------------- |
| **Phishing**       | Fake emails/messages.                  | "Your account is locked! Click here."            | Email filters, MFA, user training.    |
| **Spear Phishing** | Targeted phishing.                     | Email to CEO with personal details.              | Verify sender, limit public info.     |
| **Pretexting**     | Fabricated scenario to build trust.    | "Hi, I’m from IT. Can you reset your password?"  | Verify identities, strict procedures. |
| **Baiting**        | Offers something desirable.            | Free USB drives in parking lot.                  | User education, physical controls.    |
| **Tailgating**     | Following someone through a door.      | Unauthorized access to building.                 | Badge readers, security guards.       |
| **Quid Pro Quo**   | Offers a service in exchange for info. | "Free security scan if you share your password." | Verify requests, limit access.        |


